Routing between VDE networks with vde_router
============================================

vde_router(1) is the reference manual: it says what every option and every
management command does. This is the other half - how to get a working router,
what the pieces are for, and what to check when it does not work.

Everything here runs as an ordinary user. No tap device, no network namespace,
no root.


1. What vde_router is for
-------------------------

A VDE switch carries ethernet frames between whatever is plugged into it. Two
switches are two separate networks, and nothing crosses between them.

vde_router is what goes in the middle. It connects to two or more switches,
holds an address on each, and forwards IP between them - along with the ARP,
ICMP, DHCP and filtering that make that usable.

What makes it unusual is where its interfaces come from. They are not kernel
network devices: each one is a connection to a VDE switch, opened with the
"connect" command, and the router keeps its own ARP table, routing table and
queues for it. The kernel is not involved and no privilege is required.

You do not need it to give several machines a shared network - one switch does
that. You need it when you want more than one network and traffic between them,
or a single place to apply filtering, priorities or DHCP.


2. A first router
-----------------

Two switches with a router between them:

	vde_switch -s /tmp/sw0 -d
	vde_switch -s /tmp/sw1 -d

	cat > router.conf <<EOF
	connect /tmp/sw0
	connect /tmp/sw1
	ifconfig eth0 add 10.0.0.254 255.255.255.0
	ifconfig eth1 add 10.0.1.254 255.255.255.0
	EOF

	vde_router -c router.conf -M /tmp/router.mgmt -d

Interfaces are named in the order they are connected, so /tmp/sw0 became eth0.
Giving an address to an interface also installs the route for its network, so
the router can already forward between the two.

Check it:

	$ echo ifconfig | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/router.mgmt
	$ echo route    | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/router.mgmt

vdeterm(1) gives an interactive session on the same socket. Send one command
per connection: the router reads a command in a single operation, so a client
that writes two at once loses the second.


3. Putting hosts on the networks
--------------------------------

Anything that speaks to a VDE switch will do. Some ways:

  vdens(1) - a namespace with a real kernel stack on the switch:

	vdens vde:///tmp/sw0
	# inside:
	ip addr add 10.0.0.1/24 dev vde0
	ip link set vde0 up
	ip route add default via 10.0.0.254

  qemu with a vde netdev, if your build has one:

	qemu-system-x86_64 -netdev vde,id=n0,sock=/tmp/sw0 -device virtio-net,netdev=n0 ...

  another vde_plug, a wirefilter, a second switch - anything that speaks the
  protocol.

Give the router the DHCP server if you would rather not configure each host:

	dhcpd start eth0 10.0.0.20 10.0.0.40

The offer carries the router's address on that interface as the gateway,
together with the netmask and broadcast address derived from it.


4. Reaching the outside
-----------------------

vde_router does not do address translation, and it has no way of its own to
reach a network outside VDE. Its uplink has to be an interface like any other,
connected to something that does provide the translation.

The simplest is the slirp plugin, which is a userspace NAT. If vde_router was
built against libvdeplug4 - the default on Linux - "connect" takes a vdeplug
URL, so the uplink can live in the router process itself:

	connect /tmp/sw0
	connect slirp:///addr=10.0.2.1/dhcp=10.0.2.20
	ifconfig eth0 add 10.0.0.254 255.255.255.0
	ifconfig eth1 add 10.0.2.15 255.255.255.0
	route add default 10.0.2.1
	dhcpd start eth0 10.0.0.20 10.0.0.40

That is a complete gateway: hosts on /tmp/sw0 get an address from the router
and reach the internet through it, and no part of it needs privilege.

Note that the slirp network stays inside the router. Its own DHCP server
cannot be switched off, so keeping it on a link of its own - rather than on the
switch your hosts are attached to - is what stops it competing with the DHCP
server you actually want. If you would rather run slirp as a separate process,
give it a second switch instead:

	vde_plug vde:///tmp/sw1 slirp:///addr=10.0.2.1/dhcp=10.0.2.20

To let the outside reach in, the slirp plugin forwards ports:

	connect slirp:///addr=10.0.2.1/tcpfwd=8080:10.0.0.5:80

Anything else that bridges VDE to a real network works as an uplink too - a
switch with a tap device, for instance - but those need privilege, which is
usually the reason to prefer slirp.


5. Filtering and priorities
---------------------------

Rules are evaluated in the reverse of the order they were added, so the general
rule goes in first and the exception after it:

	ipfilter add src eth0 drop
	ipfilter add src eth0 from 10.0.0.3 255.255.255.255 accept

Besides accept, drop and reject, a rule can carry "prio", which does not decide
the fate of the packet but the queue it goes into. Each interface has an
"output" queue and thirty-two priority queues, prio0 to prio31, and each can be
given its own discipline:

	ipfilter add src eth0 tos 2 prio 3
	queue eth0:prio3 red min 80000 max 160000 probability 0.1 limit 300000
	queue eth0:output token limit 40000 bitrate 1000000

which is how bandwidth limits and IP-based QoS are expressed.


6. When it does not work
------------------------

Ask the router first. Nearly everything is visible over the management socket:

	ifconfig	are the addresses what you think they are
	route		is there a route, and out of which interface
	arp		has the next hop been resolved
	stats		are frames arriving and leaving at all

A few things are worth knowing before you go looking further.

The first packet towards a destination whose MAC the router has not yet
resolved is dropped, not held: an ARP query goes out and the sender's
retransmission gets through. A single lost packet at the start of a
conversation is normal.

ARP entries do not expire. A host that changes its MAC address while keeping
its IP - a virtual machine rebuilt with a fresh one, say - will not be reached
until the router is restarted.

The DHCP server advertises a fixed public resolver and does not send the
NetBIOS name server option. A network that needs either wants a DHCP server of
its own on the segment instead.

If frames seem not to arrive at all, look at the switch rather than the router:

	echo port/allprint | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/sw0.mgmt

and remember that a VDE socket path is a unix socket path, so it is subject to
the usual length limit - a long path fails as a plain connection refusal.


